Final Keyword in Java

-------------------------------

What is final:
- final means "cannot be changed after assignment"
- can be applied to variables, method parameters, methods, and classes
- each use has a different meaning, but the core idea is always: locked, unchangeable

-------------------------------

Final Variables (Constants):
- once assigned, the value cannot be reassigned — compile-time error if you try
- convention: name constants in ALL_CAPS with underscores

  example:
      final double PI = 3.14159;
      final int MAX_SPEED = 120;
      PI = 3.0; // compile-time error

- combined with static for true class-level constants (most common):
      static final int MAX_USERS = 100; // one value, shared, never changes

- blank final variable — declared without value, must be assigned in constructor:
      final int id;
      MyClass(int id) { this.id = id; } // assigned once, then locked

- important: final on an object reference locks the reference, NOT the object itself
      final List<String> names = new ArrayList<>();
      names.add("Alice");  // allowed — modifying the object
      names = new ArrayList<>(); // compile-time error — reassigning the reference

-------------------------------

Final Method Parameters:
- the parameter cannot be reassigned inside the method body
- does not affect the caller — only prevents accidental reassignment inside the method

  example:
      void process(final int value) {
          value = 10; // compile-time error
      }

- useful for making intent clear: "this parameter is not meant to be modified"

-------------------------------

Final Methods:
- can be inherited by subclasses but cannot be overridden
- guarantees the method's behavior stays exactly as defined in the parent class

  example:
      class Vehicle {
          final void start() { System.out.println("Starting..."); }
      }
      class Car extends Vehicle {
          void start() { ... } // compile-time error — cannot override final method
      }

- useful when a method's logic must stay consistent regardless of subclass

-------------------------------

Final Classes:
- cannot be extended (no subclass can inherit from it)
- all methods inside are implicitly final

  example:
      final class SecurityToken { ... }
      class MyToken extends SecurityToken { } // compile-time error

- real examples: String, Integer, Double — all final in Java
- used when a class must remain unchanged for security or correctness reasons

-------------------------------

Quick reference:
- final variable   → value cannot be reassigned
- final parameter  → parameter cannot be reassigned inside the method
- final method     → cannot be overridden by subclasses
- final class      → cannot be extended (inherited)